This forks off one item from #63352:

> Translate `if f(); cond { ... }` to `{ fbody; if cond { ... } }` when the inlining of f() is not a simple expression.

## Problem

`callStmt(..., unrestricted)` returns nil when a void call is `IfStmt.Init`, `ForStmt.Init`, or `SwitchStmt.Init`. Those are restricted to simple statements, so the inliner literalizes:

```go
if f(); cond { ... }
→ if func() { fbody }(); cond { ... }
```

The existing TODO in `callStmt` already describes wrapping the control statement in a block instead. `ForStmt.Post` must stay restricted: it runs every iteration, so lifting it before the loop would change semantics.

## Proposed approach

Add a restricted-init strategy for a void callee (no returns) when a binding decl is available if needed, the callee has no `defer`, and there is no label conflict:

1. Detect the call as the Init `ExprStmt` of `if`, `for`, or `switch` (not `ForStmt.Post`).
2. Replace the whole control statement with `{ fbody; if cond { ... } }` / `{ fbody; for ; cond; post { ... } }` / `{ fbody; switch tag { ... } }` — copy the control node and clear `Init`.
3. Otherwise keep literalization: `defer` in the callee, `for ;; f()` (Post), or a non-void Init that already reduces to a simple expression (`if g(); cond`).

```go
if f(); cond {}
→ println("work")
  if cond {}

for f(); cond; {}
→ println("work")
  for cond {}

switch f(); x {}
→ println("work")
  switch x {}

for ; ; f() {}          // Post: not this strategy
→ for ; ; func() { println("work") }() {}

if f(); cond {}         // callee uses defer
→ if func() { defer println("cleanup"); println("work") }(); cond {}
```

