The following is an LLM-written summary; I have not verified its validity, but it seems plausible. This issue was reported to us as a potential security bug. Image misparsing is actually the canonical example of "not every bug is a security bug" in our [security decisions](https://go.dev/doc/security/decisions#principles).

In `golang.org/x/image/vp8l`, `(*hTree).buildSimple` (`vp8l/huffman.go:184-196`) constructs a Huffman tree for 1- or 2-symbol "simple prefix codes" (RFC 9649 §3.7.2.1.1) by assigning codeword `i` in bitstream read order:

```go
func (h *hTree) buildSimple(nSymbols uint32, symbols [2]uint32, alphabetSize uint32) error {
    h.nodes = make([]hNode, 1, 2*nSymbols-1)
    for i := uint32(0); i < nSymbols; i++ {
        if symbols[i] >= alphabetSize {
            return errInvalidHuffmanTree
        }
        if err := h.insert(symbols[i], i, nSymbols-1); err != nil {
            return err
        }
    }
    return nil
}
```

Per RFC 9649 §3.7.2.1.1 and libwebp (ReadHuffmanCode in src/dec/vp8l_dec.c + BuildHuffmanTable in src/utils/huffman_utils.c), the simple prefix code header populates code_lengths[symbol0] = 1 and (when nSymbols == 2) code_lengths[symbol1] = 1, and then builds a canonical Huffman table ordered by ascending symbol value. Because buildSimple uses read order directly instead of canonical order, it diverges from libwebp in two cases when nSymbols == 2:

1. symbols[0] > symbols[1]: buildSimple assigns codeword 0 to symbols[0] and codeword 1 to symbols[1], whereas canonical ordering assigns codeword 0 to the smaller symbol (symbols[1]) and 1 to the larger symbol (symbols[0]), inverting decoded symbol values.
2. symbols[0] == symbols[1]: code_lengths has only one distinct symbol with a non-zero code  length, which libwebp collapses into a 0-bit single-symbol tree (consuming 0 bits per decoded symbol). buildSimple instead builds a 1-bit tree with two identical leaves, consuming 1 bit per decoded symbol and desynchronizing the bit reader

This was originally reported to us as a security issue. We do not believe that this is a vulnerability, and are therefore filing it as a regular issue.

